 ## Queue and Tickets
 
+### Queue
+
+A **queue** belongs to one entity and has a `name`, a `slug` unique within the entity, an `is_active` flag (default: active) and optional `labels_i18n` (per-locale labels, JSON). Inactive queues do not issue tickets.
+
 ### Ticket
 
-Unit of service in the queue. It is **single-use** and receives **one single call** — all data is immutable after issuance, except `called_at` and `called_by` (filled when called). Attributes:
+Unit of service in the queue. It is **single-use** and receives **one single call** — all data is immutable after issuance, except `status`, `called_at` and `called_by` (filled when called). Attributes:
 
 - Source queue — the ticket belongs to **one** queue (within the entity); does not record which totem it came from.
+- Display `code` (see below).
 - Priority (numeric, see below).
 - Issuance timestamp (ordering "oldest first" within same priority).
-- Issuance channel: totem, virtual, printed copy, etc.
+- Issuance channel: `totem`, `virtual` or `printed` (default `virtual`).
+- Status: `waiting` (on issuance) or `called`. There is no cancelled status.
+
+**Display code:** per-queue sequence `A001`, `A002`, … — prefix `A`, at least 3 digits, zero padded; beyond 999 it grows (`A1000`, `A1001`). The next code is the last valid code + 1 in insertion order, so `A999` followed by `A1000` yields `A1001`. Codes not matching `A<digits>` are skipped (with only `A-X` present the next is `A001`). Deleting a ticket that is not the latest one does not free its number (after A001..A003 with A002 deleted, the next is A004). Deleting the latest ticket makes its number the next one again, since no counter is kept. The code is unique per queue.
+
+**Issuance:** the action checks the queue state in the database while holding the queue row lock — a queue deactivated after the caller loaded it is rejected, and nothing is persisted on rejection. A negative priority is rejected. A concurrent collision on the code is retried a few times.
+
+**Call:** only members of the ticket's entity may call (others are denied, the ticket is unchanged). A ticket can be called once; calling a ticket that is not waiting is rejected. Which ticket to call next (priority and fairness) is a separate rule, not yet implemented.
 
 ### Priority
 
